<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drohiczyn</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dorohychyn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Drohiczyn is a small town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-bug">Bug River</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, once an important stronghold on the borders of Rus', Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. In 1253 Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> was crowned King of Rus' there by a papal legate. It later became the chief town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podlachia">Podlachia</a> region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drohiczyn.jpg" alt="Drohiczyn" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zala · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drohiczyn_Zesp%C3%B3%C5%82_klasztorny_jezuit%C3%B3w_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Drohiczyn stands on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-bug">Bug River</a> in Siemiatycze County, Podlaskie Voivodeship. Traces of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlement, La Tène crematoria and ancient graves have been found there, and in ancient times the site lay among dense forests. It is regarded as one of the oldest towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podlachia">Podlachia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the area was inhabited by the Yotvingians, and the fortified settlement was most likely theirs; Rus' chronicles mention it in 1061. In 1142 Grand Duke Vsevolod II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> gave Drohiczyn, as Dorohychin, and Brest to his younger brother Igor. In 1192 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-ii-the-just">Casimir II the Just</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a>, Yotvingians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> near the town, and in 1237 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a> handed it to the Order of Dobrzyń, and Prince Daniel annexed it to Galicia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> in 1238. After the Mongol invasion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podlachia">Podlasie</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> in 1241, but the Rus' dukes regained Drohiczyn. In 1253 Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, strengthened by a victorious war against the Yotvingians, was crowned King of Ruthenia there.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> captured the town again in 1274, and it remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569, apart from the 1380s and 1430s, when Duke Janusz of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> held it. It was among the major cities of the Grand Duchy and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights in 1498. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a> it became capital of Podlaskie Voivodeship, established in 1513, and prospered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">The Deluge</a> of 1655-1660 brought looting by Swedes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>, and on 3 May 1657 the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-rakoczi">George II Rákóczi</a> captured the town, killed most residents and destroyed the parish church and castle; Drohiczyn never recovered its greatness.</p><p>In 1795 the third partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> divided the town between the Habsburg Empire and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> along the Bug, and a fire in 1805 destroyed the town hall's archives. In 1939 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> occupied it; residents were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and houses near the river were moved or destroyed, along with two churches. German forces took it in June 1941, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of the town were sent in autumn 1942 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treblinka-extermination-camp">Treblinka</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Drohiczyn, with a population of 2,110, has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> churches and monasteries, regional and diocesan museums and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s only Canoeing Museum. Since 1991 it has been the seat of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Drohiczyn.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Drohiczyn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Daniel submitted to Batu Khan in 1245 but looked to the West for help, and in 1253 he was crowned King of Rus' by a papal legate at <b>Dorohychyn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-volhynia">Kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia</a></li></ul></section>
